# Round 2 — Interpretation of the Null & One Refined Test

Math/pattern expert. Inputs: Round-1 engine (`similarity_engine_design.md`), run result: 0/1688 cross-domain pairs interesting; WY null min-statistic degenerate at D=0; enrichment p=0.55; exploratory D ∈ {0, 0.091, 0.195}.

---

## 1. Interpretation: the degenerate null IS the result — a non-identifiability theorem, not a null finding

### 1.1 The mechanism, exactly

Let φ: items → profiles be the Round-1 encoding, and L ∈ {crop, Klüver, motif, monument} the domain label (sizes 60/4/8/13, N=85). The confirmatory statistic is T = min over cross-label pairs of D(i,j) = min over cross-label pairs of ‖φ(i)−φ(j)‖_Gower.

**Observation 1 (collisions are structurally guaranteed).** φ's effective image is tiny and its pushforward distribution is heavily concentrated. Decorative geometry lives almost entirely on {C_n, D_n : n ∈ {3,4,5,6,8,10,12}} ∪ {concentric (∞)} ∪ {spiral}, with octave equivalence (6≈12≈24) collapsing it further, × ≤ 8 topology-flag combos × a 6-point spiky ratio signature. The continuous-looking components (FD, ratio_sig) are near-functions of the discrete ones (F_k peaks at k = n_fold by construction — §2.4 of Round 1). With ~40 effective cells and 85 items drawn from a Zipf-like concentration on highly-composite orders, E[#duplicate profile pairs] ≈ 470 (Monte Carlo, Zipf s=1.2); even under far more generous cell counts, birthday-paradox collisions are overdetermined. **The encoding is non-injective across domains by construction, not by coincidence.**

**Observation 2 (one collision kills the test).** Under label shuffling with sizes preserved, any fixed pair is cross-label with probability

> P(cross) = 1 − Σ_c s_c(s_c−1) / N(N−1) = 1 − 3764/7140 = **0.473**.

Hence a **single** exact-duplicate pair — and one was observed: dumbbell (1,1) ↔ chamber axial (1,1), D=0 — puts an atom of mass ≈0.47 at the global metric minimum 0 in the Westfall–Young null {T_b}. The α=0.05 quantile of {T_b} is then 0; no pair can have D < 0; and the D=0 pair itself has p_pair ≈ (1+0.47B)/(B+1) ≈ 0.47. **The test's power function is identically zero against every alternative.** Zero interesting pairs is therefore a *theorem about the procedure*, deducible before looking at the data — not an empirical finding.

### 1.2 The precise statement

> **Proposition (decorative-space non-identifiability).** Let φ be any feature map whose profile space has ν effective cells with ν = O(10¹) and whose items concentrate on low, highly-composite symmetry orders, and let domains be arbitrary labelings of N=85 items. Then (i) cross-domain D=0 collisions occur w.p. → 1 as concentration grows; (ii) any min-D permutation test (Westfall–Young on label shuffles) has power = 0, because its null support minimum equals the metric minimum and carries mass ≥ 0.473; (iii) consequently no conclusion about cross-domain resemblance — positive OR negative — is identifiable from (φ, min-D, label-shuffle) at these N. The quantity I(L; φ) is bounded above by H(L) = 1.30 bits, but the effective resolution of φ at the mode of the distribution (orders 4–12, where ~all mass sits) is ≪ log2(85) ≈ 6.4 bits, so the many-to-one map swamps the label information.

In words: **the decorative-pattern space, at the resolution at which humans can legibly encode it, is so small and so concentrated at low symmetry orders that "cross-domain matching" is content-free — the hypothesis (transmission/convergence) has finer grain than the measurement.** That is the interesting mathematical result. It is an *upper bound on what is knowable from this feature class*, not evidence that the domains are unrelated.

### 1.3 What the result is NOT (guardrails against over-reading)

- **Not a substantive null.** A degenerate null (power ≡ 0) must be reported as *uninformative*, which is epistemically different from "test had power and found nothing." The enrichment test (p=0.55) has a non-degenerate count statistic and IS a weak substantive null — but it averages over 1688 pairs, so it only rules out *broad* cross-domain closeness, not a handful of real matches.
- **Not fixable by more permutations or bigger B.** The atom at 0 is a property of the collision graph, invariant to B.
- **Partially fixable by resolution.** Raising ν (continuous FD on real imagery, sub-symmetry spectra {F_k} rather than argmax n_fold, full Bessel-mode spectra instead of dominant (n,m)) shrinks the D=0 atom — but for *idealized glyphs* of canonical motifs (Flower of Life, rose window) the FD is nearly a function of n_fold, so collisions among canonical forms persist. The degeneracy is a property of the pattern class, not only of the encoder: **low-order dihedral symmetry classes are finite and shared by all ornamental traditions on Earth.** Any purely geometric cross-sectional test inherits this ceiling.

### 1.4 Reclassification of the exploratory matches

- dumbbell↔chamber D=0: an encoding collision (both (n,m)=(1,1) + shared flags). Content-free, per §1.2.
- 12-spoke wheel↔Chartres rose D=0.091: driven by n=12 ∧ concentric — the two most populated cells in the space. Under a non-degenerate statistic this is exactly the pair type with the *highest* null probability; assign no weight.
- Koch↔Flower of Life D=0.195: only mildly interesting because Koch (is_grid/lattice + recursion) vs FoL (concentric + 6-fold) disagree on topology flags, so the match is not a pure collision — but it survives no multiplicity layer. Log as hypothesis-generating only (Round-1 HARKing firewall, item 4).

